  - title: About me
    description: I am a researcher exploring how experience and emotion shape cognitive and interpersonal functioning. My MA thesis investigates how affective context and individual differences shape perception and decision-making, including perceptual bias, stability, and processing speed, with implications for how people interpret and respond to emotionally salient experiences.
I look forward to, one day, continuing my education in a PhD program where I can better understand how cognitive and emotional processes contribute to long-term psychosocial outcomes in individuals and families affected by adversity. Ultimately, I hope to apply this understanding to promote resilience and adaptive functioning among populations experiencing trauma, affective difficulties, or disrupted memory processes like can be experienced in the military.
Through previous research experiences rooted in trauma, psychopathology, and emotion-related mechanisms in diverse populations across the life span, I have come to appreciate the complex interplay between experience, cognition, and affect in shaping behavior and relationships over time. 

Outside of the lab, I am an avid crocheter, visual artist and self-taught musician who enjoys painting as well as playing music on a variety of insturments (most often flute and guitar!).
